WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.234
"Who Is Rescuing Whom - Pets and Longevity?"

00:00:07.325 --> 00:00:11.360
Intense social connectivity
is a Blue Zone attribute

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.320
scrutinized for
its potential role

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.800
in supporting longevity.

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:18.080
Does slobbery
social contact count?

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:19.760
More than two-thirds
of U.S. households

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.840
include a pet (including mine!).

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:25.160
In an "awwww"-inspiring paper

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.560
published in the prestigious
journal Science entitled

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.360
"Oxytocin-gaze positive loop

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:32.040
and the coevolution
of human-dog bonds,"

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.960
researchers found that petting
or looking into the eyes

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:38.120
of a canine companion
leads to oxytocin release

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.400
in the brains of both
the humans and the dogs,

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.560
the same "love hormone" that
bonds breastfeeding mothers

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:45.080
to their infants.

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.120
I was reading about
the potential mechanisms

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.880
by which having
an animal companion

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.000
might improve survival
after a heart attack

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.400
when I ran across a passage

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.200
about a "profound"
cardiovascular response

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.280
when petting dogs or horses.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.080
"This response
usually takes the form

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:00.560
of a significant reduction

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.920
in the heart rate
and blood pressure."

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:04.720
I could totally see that.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.120
But then the next sentence
made me do a double take:

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.920
"Unfortunately,
we have no information

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.440
about
the physiological responses

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:14.080
of the person
doing the petting."

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.240
Oh, so they were talking
about the heart rate

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.080
and blood pressure
of the animals!

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.520
To my surprise,
studies of the effects

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.960
of companion animals
on human health have,

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.560
as one review put it,

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.200
produced a "mishmash
of conflicting results."

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:30.800
But a meta-analysis of studies

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:32.760
involving more than
three million people

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:34.600
followed for an average
of a decade

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.320
found that
canine companionship,

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:37.240
for example,

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:39.480
was associated with
a 24 percent reduction

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.760
in the risk
of premature death.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.560
The authors suggested
that enhanced survival

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.880
may be a result
of increased physical activity

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:48.640
from walking the dog.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.800
That could also help explain
why pet ownership

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:54.000
has been associated
with a lower risk of frailty.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.240
Of course, this too
could be reverse causation,

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.680
where people
who are already active

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.640
are more likely to get a dog
in the first place,

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.400
but the greater issue
may be confounding factors.

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.720
Pet owners tend to be
younger, wealthier,

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:11.600
more educated,
and more married.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.520
Sadly, when these types
of elements

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:14.880
are taken into account,

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.600
the significant mortality risk
reduction disappears.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:21.880
The same appears to be true
for pet ownership in general.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.280
The higher survival rate

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.360
becomes
statistically insignificant

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.680
once confounding factors,
like socioeconomic factors,

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:29.760
are taken into account.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:33.360
When it comes specifically
to cats,

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.480
confounding can work
in the opposite direction.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.280
Given evidence
that social support

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.520
may help with survival
after a heart attack,

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.080
a number of studies
have been undertaken

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.320
to see if pet ownership
can help.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.880
One study found
that cat owners

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.160
had a significantly
greater risk of death.

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:50.640
This may be
because they appear

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.200
to not only be more likely
to be sedentary

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.000
than people with dogs,
who may be going on more walks,

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:59.400
but cat owners
may also be more sedentary

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.000
than people
without any pets at all.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.320
Given the pitfalls
of trying to impute

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.720
cause-and-effect
from observational studies,

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.440
I was so excited to see
a randomized controlled trial

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.280
that actually put pet ownership
to the test.

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:17.240
Don't get your hopes
up too much, though.

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:18.000
The title was,

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.400
"Effect of Pet Insects
on the Psychological Health

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:23.640
of Community-Dwelling
Elderly People:

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.800
A Single-Blinded, Randomized,
Controlled Trial."

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.680
The "pets" were crickets.

00:03:30.520 --> 00:03:32.200
But compared to
the control group,

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.480
older individuals
(average age 72)

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.840
randomized to take care
of crickets

00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:39.360
experienced a significantly
greater improvement

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.440
in depression,
cognitive status,

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.080
and mental quality of life.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:47.080
So, maybe there's hope
for Fido yet.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.600
Most studies on pets
and human health

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:50.480
are snapshot-in-time studies

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.240
where it's impossible to know
which came first,

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.760
but longitudinal studies
over time have shown

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.280
that dog walkers
are indeed more likely

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.760
to achieve
recommended activity levels.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:04.040
Sadly, only a minority--

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:06.840
only about a third--
of older dog owners

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:10.360
were found to even walk
their dogs three times a week.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:13.880
To maximize the benefits
for pooch and person alike,

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:15.080
here's wise advice

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:18.960
from a medical journal article
published in 1925:

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.680
"The best prescription
to be written for a walk

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:29.600
is to take a dog..."

